\section{Feed-Forward Neural Network using Keras and TensorFlow}

After completing the course setup, load the MNIST handwritten-digit dataset using \texttt{keras3} with the TensorFlow backend. Scale the pixel values from $[0,255]$ to $[0,1]$ once. Keep the images as arrays of shape $(n,28,28)$; the network's \texttt{layer\_flatten()} layer will flatten each image.

<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(keras3)
use_backend("tensorflow")
mnist <- dataset_mnist()
mnist$train$x <- mnist$train$x/255
mnist$test$x <- mnist$test$x/255
@

\emph{Data and metrics.} Use validation data for model selection and error analysis; reserve the MNIST test set for Task~\ref{q:a3:test-evaluation}. Keras holds out the last 30\% of training observations before shuffling when using \texttt{validation\_split = 0.3}; see the tutorial. Loss means sparse categorical cross-entropy; classification error is $1-\mathrm{accuracy}$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) As a first step, we visualize a couple of digits. Include the first eight you can find in the training dataset in the report as an image.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
idx <- 3
im <- mnist$train$x[idx,,]
# For upright digits, reverse MNIST's top-to-bottom rows to
# match image()'s upward y-axis, then transpose (columns=x, rows=y).
im <- t(apply(im, 2, rev))
image(1:28, 1:28, im, col=gray((0:255)/255), xlab = "", ylab = "",
        xaxt='n', yaxt='n', main=paste(mnist$train$y[idx]))
@
\item (1p) How large is the training and test set?
\item (2p) Implement a feed-forward network in R using \texttt{keras3}; see the \href{https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/blob/master/additional_material/supplementary_r_files/tensorflow_feedforward_neural_network_tutorial.R}{Keras 3 feed-forward tutorial}. Use one hidden layer with 16 sigmoid units and a softmax output layer. Include the model description in your report.

Call \texttt{set\_random\_seed(123)} before constructing the model. Train with SGD, learning rate 0.05, no momentum, 3 epochs, batch size 128, and 30\% validation data. Keep the epoch count, batch size, and validation split fixed for the next task.
\begin{enumerate}
\item How many parameters do your model have in total?
\item How many parameters does the input layer have?
\item How many parameters does the output layer have?
\item\label{q:a3:baseline-curves} Report the final epoch's validation accuracy and validation loss. Include the training and validation accuracy and loss curves produced by \texttt{plot(history)}.

\emph{Hint!} With these settings, the final validation accuracy should be roughly 0.84. Small differences between runs and software versions are expected.

\end{enumerate}

\item\label{q:a3:model-variants} (2p) Make the following cumulative changes. For each variant, reset the seed to 123 before constructing and compiling a new model with fresh weights. Keep 3 epochs, batch size 128, and the same validation split. Report the baseline and all six variants in one comparison table with final-epoch validation accuracy and loss. Include learning curves for the baseline and final variant (reuse Task~\ref{q:a3:baseline-curves}), plus the final model description. Give one brief interpretation of each change, including any decrease in performance.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Increase the number of hidden units to 128.
\item Change the activation function to reLU.
\item Change the optimizer to Adam (as in Assignment 1), using learning rate 0.001.
\item Add a second layer with 128 hidden units.
\item Add dropout with 0.2 dropout probability for both of your hidden layers.
\item Add batch normalization for both of your hidden layers.
\end{enumerate}
\item (2p) Conceptually describe how you would use early stopping as regularization. What would you monitor (e.g.\ \texttt{val\_loss}), in which direction should it improve, and would you retain the final epoch's weights or restore those from the best epoch? Explain your choices.

\emph{Note!} No implementation or additional training is required for this task.
\item\label{q:a3:additional-models} (2p) Start from the best-performing configuration in Task~\ref{q:a3:model-variants} and try \emph{one to three additional feed-forward configurations}. Briefly motivate each choice. Train each candidate from fresh weights for \emph{at most 20 epochs}, using the same validation split; early stopping is optional. Add their validation accuracy and loss to the comparison table from Task~\ref{q:a3:model-variants}. Describe your selected architecture and include its final \texttt{keras3} code and training and validation curves. Explain your choice, including if none of the candidates improves on Task~\ref{q:a3:model-variants}.

\emph{Hint!} The general idea is to find the optimal capacity of the model. Try to start with a network that overfits the data and then regularize that model to improve the generalization error on the validation set.

\newpage
\emph{Optional early stopping for Task~\ref{q:a3:additional-models}.} This \href{https://keras3.posit.co/reference/callback_early_stopping.html}{Keras 3 callback} monitors validation loss (lower is better) and stops when validation loss no longer improves:
<<early-stopping-example,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
early_stop <- callback_early_stopping(
  monitor = "val_loss", mode = "min",
  restore_best_weights = TRUE
)
# In fit(...), add: callbacks = list(early_stop)
@
With \texttt{restore\_best\_weights = TRUE}, the model retains weights from the epoch with the lowest validation loss; \texttt{FALSE} (the default) keeps the final epoch's weights. Evaluate the retained model on the validation data to report its accuracy and loss: the final history entry may refer to different weights. The 20-epoch limit still applies.

\item (2p) Identify two digits from the \emph{validation set} that your selected network has classified incorrectly. Visualize them and include the true digit and the predicted digit. Why do you think the network made each classification mistake?

\item\label{q:a3:test-evaluation} (1p) After model selection, evaluate your selected model once on the MNIST test set. Report test accuracy and loss, compare with validation metrics for the same model, and discuss possible reasons for differences. Is test accuracy necessarily lower than validation accuracy? Explain.

\end{enumerate}


