\section{* A simple neural network from scratch}

\input{distinction_task.tex}

We will implement a small feed-forward network for the exclusive-or (XOR) function. For two binary inputs, the target is 1 when exactly one input is 1, and 0 otherwise. A nonlinear hidden layer allows the network to represent this pattern.

The example is adapted from \citet[Section~6.1]{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}. All details needed for this exercise are given below; no reading of that section is required. For background, follow the \href{https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1HC_QN2mCq9bkCPzmkP8RaR3RokFQCWo9oPuU7rFyR8Y/edit?usp=sharing}{general reading instructions for Block 3}.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Implement a network with two inputs, two hidden units, and one output as a function \texttt{mini\_net(X, W, c, w, b)}. Its forward computation is
\[
H = \operatorname{ReLU}(XW+c), \qquad \widehat{y}=Hw+b,
\]
where $\operatorname{ReLU}(a)=\max(0,a)$ is applied separately to every element, and the scalar output bias $b$ is added to every prediction. Use the supplied parameter values to compute predictions; no training step is required. For $n$ observations, use the following shapes, with one observation per row:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\textbf{Object} & \textbf{Meaning} & \textbf{R shape} \\
\hline
\texttt{X} & Input observations & $n \times 2$ \\
\texttt{W} & Input-to-hidden weights & $2 \times 2$ \\
\texttt{c} & Hidden-layer biases, repeated across rows & $n \times 2$ \\
\texttt{w} & Hidden-to-output weights & $2 \times 1$ \\
\texttt{b} & Output bias & Scalar \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The hidden-layer bias vector has two components. In this R example, \texttt{c} repeats those same two values in every row so that it can be added to the hidden-layer inputs. With four observations, \texttt{c} is a $4 \times 2$ matrix representing \emph{two shared biases}. Use $n$ rows when changing the number of observations.

\emph{Debugging hint!} Check \texttt{dim()} after each matrix multiplication and bias addition. The hidden-layer matrices should be $n \times 2$, and the predictions should be $n \times 1$.

Here is an example:

<<echo = FALSE>>=
mini_net <- function(X, W, c, w, b){
    matrix(c(0,1,1,0))
}
@

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
W <- matrix(1, nrow = 2, ncol = 2)
c <- matrix(c(0, -1), ncol = 2, nrow = 4, byrow = TRUE)
X <- matrix(c(0,0,1,1,0,1,0,1), ncol = 2)
w <- matrix(c(1, -2), ncol = 1)
b <- 0

mini_net(X, W, c, w, b)
@

<<echo = FALSE>>=
mini_net <- function(X, W, c, w, b){
    matrix(c(0,0.9,0.9,0.2))
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval = TRUE>>=
mini_net(X, W*0.9, c, w, b)
@

\item (1p) Now test changing the value $W_{1,1}$ to 0. What do you get for the results of the network?
\item (1p) What is the current output function? Is the network output function reasonable? Do you have any other output function that might be better?
\item (1p) Now implement a mean squared error loss function for the network. It should work as follows.
<<echo = FALSE>>=
mini_net_loss <- function(y, X, W, c, w, b){
    return(0)
}
@
<<eval = TRUE>>=
y <- c(0,1,1,0)
mini_net_loss(y, X, W, c, w, b)
@

<<echo = FALSE>>=
mini_net_loss <- function(y, X, W, c, w, b){
    return(0.015)
}
@
<<eval = TRUE>>=
mini_net_loss(y, X, 0.9*W, c, w, b)
@

\item (1p) Again, change the value $W_{1,1}$ to 0. What is the value of the loss function?
%\item Now we are going to train our mini network using gradient descent. The gradient for the ReLU function is
%\[
%\frac{\partial \text{ReLU}(x)}{\partial x} =
%\]
%Use this to implement the gradient for $\mathcal{W}$ (we keep the other parameters fixed). Remember the chain rule of calculus (see Chapter XX). You can derive this analytically for each of the four values of W.
%\item initialize values W using the distribution in XXX eq. 1. What is the initialization distribution.
%\item use batch/full gradient descent to train your mini network to find roughly the same parameter values as above. Visualize the loss per epoch/iteration as you did in assignment 1.
\end{enumerate}


